WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.519
Straighten the contour contributions.

00:00:02.519 --> 00:00:05.286
Follow four successive arcs of the inner ellipse.

00:00:05.286 --> 00:00:10.065
For each arc integrate the complex density, including its oriented tangent.

00:00:10.065 --> 00:00:15.506
The four coloured output arrows are the resulting increments, delta one through delta four.

00:00:15.506 --> 00:00:16.929
Join them head to tail.

00:00:16.929 --> 00:00:24.567
The gold displacement measures the distance from the first tail to the last tip, about zero point zero four nine four two.

00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:27.512
Now straighten the four arrows, preserving their lengths.

00:00:27.512 --> 00:00:34.105
The violet total is about zero point three one five because the directed arrows had partly cancelled.

00:00:34.105 --> 00:00:40.783
The mint bar measures the length of the entire curved output path, including the bends within each of the four arcs.

00:00:40.783 --> 00:00:48.307
On each arc the chord is at most the path length, so adding the four chords gives at most the integral of the absolute density.

00:00:48.307 --> 00:00:50.587
Replace the local heights by ceilings.

00:00:50.587 --> 00:00:56.988
At each angle, taking the modulus changes the contour density into a product of three real heights.

00:00:56.988 --> 00:01:01.874
Violet is the magnitude of the function at the moving point on the inner ellipse.

00:01:01.874 --> 00:01:06.355
Its horizontal ceiling is the supremum over the outer ellipse, M rho.

00:01:06.355 --> 00:01:10.708
Pink is the magnitude of the degree two kernel on that same inner contour.

00:01:10.708 --> 00:01:13.546
Its ceiling is the maximum around that contour.

00:01:13.546 --> 00:01:17.493
Mint is the speed, the distance along the ellipse per unit angle.

00:01:17.493 --> 00:01:21.974
Multiply speed by a small angle step to obtain a small arc length.

00:01:21.974 --> 00:01:26.796
Summing those lengths around one turn gives the mint area, the perimeter L sub s.

00:01:26.796 --> 00:01:33.069
Take the first two ceilings outside the integral and keep the perimeter, giving the bound below for any degree n.

00:01:33.069 --> 00:01:36.376
This is the supremum estimate of Lemma one point nine.

00:01:36.376 --> 00:01:43.645
Lesson seven uses Lemma one point ten, where Cauchy Schwarz instead separates two angular mean squares.

00:01:43.645 --> 00:01:45.818
Every second frequency survives.

00:01:45.818 --> 00:01:49.979
Switch from the degree two contour example to the kernel of degree three.

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:56.017
Lesson five leaves frequencies four, six, eight, and then every second positive index.

00:01:56.017 --> 00:02:02.311
The numbers c four, c six and c eight are the lengths of the first three complex arrows on this contour.

00:02:02.311 --> 00:02:04.167
Each length has two ingredients.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:11.805
Sigma is the coefficient found by integrating the third Legendre polynomial of cosine t against sine of k t.

00:02:11.805 --> 00:02:15.796
At frequency four this integral is thirty-two over thirty-five.

00:02:15.796 --> 00:02:24.394
Multiply it by the circle radius one point five four to the power minus four to get about zero point one six two five five.

00:02:24.394 --> 00:02:28.576
The identical rule gives the pink and violet lengths for six and eight.

00:02:28.576 --> 00:02:33.547
The remaining exponential has modulus one and rotates each arrow clockwise.

00:02:33.547 --> 00:02:41.441
Above each dial the wave shows the real projection of that arrow on the ellipse, enlarged separately so all three waves are visible.

00:02:41.441 --> 00:02:44.620
A full turn gives four, six and eight oscillations.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:49.692
Adding their lengths and all the later lengths bounds the complete kernel at any angle.

00:02:49.692 --> 00:02:51.780
Sum the geometric majorant.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:55.514
For degree n, the first surviving frequency is n plus one.

00:02:55.514 --> 00:02:58.693
The index q counts steps of two, starting at zero.

00:02:58.693 --> 00:03:04.049
Take absolute values of the waves and bound each sigma by two using Lesson five.

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:07.911
The first three upper bounds are the blue, pink and violet blocks.

00:03:07.911 --> 00:03:14.013
Dividing any block by its predecessor cancels the initial power and leaves one over s squared.

00:03:14.013 --> 00:03:18.728
This number is less than one because the inner circle radius s exceeds one.

00:03:18.728 --> 00:03:24.211
The entire strip has length equal to the first block divided by one minus the ratio.

00:03:24.211 --> 00:03:32.276
Multiply the numerator and denominator by s to obtain two times s to the minus n over s minus its reciprocal.

00:03:32.276 --> 00:03:36.415
Put that kernel bound into the pink slot of the coefficient estimate.

00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:40.363
Its factor of two cancels the two in the contour normalization.

00:03:40.363 --> 00:03:44.844
Group the perimeter and the remaining denominator into C infinity of s.

00:03:44.844 --> 00:03:50.363
The factor s to the minus n stays visible because it controls decay with degree.

